onal research suggesting a  strong association between  sugar intake and type 2 diabetes even  after controlling for  total calorie intake body weight alcohol  consumption  and exercise if we look at this directly  this association might be explained by  the idea that  excessive fructose intake may increase  inflammation in the body fatty liver and  localized  insulin resistance but then the other  indirect argument is related to weight  and i want to take a quick second here  to unpack that  obviously eating a diet rich in free  sugars without any fiber to help  slow down its absorption is not going to  be  very satiating and may therefore be  really easy to overeat so then the  theory follows that consuming an excess  of calories  leads to weight gain and body fat does  make it harder for the  insulin to make its way into the cells  however  this is actually just one small part of  a very complicated puzzle  and in fact insulin resistance also  increases body fatness because of its  role as a growth hormone  also with regards to the sugar argument  starving insulin resistant cells  don't cry out for broccoli they call out  for straight-up sugar  in other words it's a two-way street  here so  it's really important not to put the  blame on the individual  and their lifestyle choices especially  since losing weight without addressing  some of the other causes of  insulin resistance like stress poor  sleep  saturated fat intake medications  poor pain management and physical  inactivity  may not fully fix the problem so people  with type 2 diabetes come in a wide  range of body sizes  and shapes many of whom eat a generally  balanced diet  so we really should not assume that  somebody is going to get diabetes  because they eat a lot of  junk food or that they're going to avoid  diabetes because they eat  clean diabetes is a huge complicated  topic so  this is just really scratching the  surface here but if you want  more details leave me a comment below  having said  a